WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560
Speaker 1: Once Donald Trump got indicted and&nbsp;
then arrested as a result of the New York hush&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:09.300
money payment investigation, the consensus among&nbsp;
political wonks became that his approval numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:13.980
at least among Republicans, would actually go&nbsp;
up. And this is, of course, because Republicans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:19.200
love claiming to be the victim and they love to&nbsp;
pacify Donald Trump when he plays the victim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:23.640
when he goes around the country and acts like,&nbsp;
oh, everyone's just trying to get me and every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:27.600
investigation into me isn't actually because I&nbsp;
committed any wrongdoing. It's all just part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:33.360
a. Craddock hit piece. It's a witch hunt and it's&nbsp;
all very unfair. Despite, of course, Republicans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.700
claiming to be the people who support rule of&nbsp;
law, law and order, personal responsibility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:44.520
not acting like a fragile special snowflake. All&nbsp;
these other things, they do make an exception to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.360
these rules when it comes to their dear leader,&nbsp;
Donald Trump. So the prediction was that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:52.980
indictment slash arrest news was actually going to&nbsp;
help Donald Trump in the upcoming primary battle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:57.900
And it seems like that's actually playing out&nbsp;
because we now have a new poll from Reuters Ipsos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:04.440
which shows that Donald Trump is at 58% support in&nbsp;
the Republican primary, with Ron DeSantis all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:11.700
way down at 21%. That's 37% down, which is quite&nbsp;
astounding. Now, we know that the Santos tends to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:17.340
do better in these poll matchups when he is in&nbsp;
a one on one competition against Donald Trump,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:22.200
since his support doesn't get diluted amongst&nbsp;
these other candidates who don't really have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:26.160
chance realistically. But at least as it pertains&nbsp;
to this poll, it doesn't seem to have that much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:32.160
of an effect, because in this poll poll, we see&nbsp;
Donald Trump at 58%, we see DeSantis at 21%. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.660
then all the way down at the bottom of the list,&nbsp;
we see Mike Pence at 4%. He probably shouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:40.980
even bother to run at this point. I don't see what&nbsp;
reason he would want to get in this race for Nikki&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:45.600
Haley is at a pathetic 1%, even though she is&nbsp;
officially running. We have Liz Cheney, who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.520
at 3%. She may run, but my guess is that if she&nbsp;
does decide to run, it would be as an independent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:55.620
to sabotage a potential Trump candidacy rather&nbsp;
than to run as a Republican where she'd have no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.620 --> 00:02:01.740
shot at the at the primary. We have Mike Pompeo,&nbsp;
Vivek Ramaswamy and Glenn Youngkin, who all have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:08.520
1% support, and then Kristi Noem, Chris Christie,&nbsp;
Tim Scott and Chris Sununu all are at 0%. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:14.160
this poll is particularly good for Trump. His&nbsp;
58% is up from the same poll that had him at 48%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:20.040
earlier this month and 44% from the middle of&nbsp;
March. We, of course, can never glean too much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:25.800
from one poll and we tend to see regression back&nbsp;
to the mean. But it is important to remember that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:30.900
more charges may soon be coming against Donald&nbsp;
Trump, particularly out of Georgia and maybe even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:36.780
from Washington, D.C. If that's the case, Donald&nbsp;
Trump may be able to build up on his momentum and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:41.640
this race could be over before it even starts.&nbsp;
The first primaries aren't until February of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:46.500
next year, so that's like ten months away from&nbsp;
now and there's plenty of time remaining. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:52.320
with the Santos currently so far behind, he may&nbsp;
get scared out of announcing altogether. He may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:59.160
assess that it's just better for him to wait until&nbsp;
2028 or 2032 if he wants a real shot at winning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:05.520
this thing. Because at that point, Donald Trump&nbsp;
is not going to be in the picture anymore. And as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:09.840
long as he can continue to stay relevant in the&nbsp;
world of politics, keep doing what he's doing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:15.840
Florida, maybe he'll be able to still be relevant&nbsp;
and run a successful campaign in a future election&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.940
cycle. Of course, the Santos seem to have a real&nbsp;
shot at winning this thing following the 2022&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:26.820
primaries when he way outperformed the Trumpian&nbsp;
candidates. But I believe that his star may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:32.400
peaked too early, and now it's Donald Trump's turn&nbsp;
once again. We also don't suspect that DeSantis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:38.040
would do what it takes to take down Trump, which&nbsp;
is to go at him forcefully and directly. He'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:43.440
likely be tepid for as long as possible until&nbsp;
he has absolutely no choice but to go after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:49.200
Donald Trump in desperation once it's already too&nbsp;
late. That's what we saw took place in 2016 in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.120
Republican primary. I remember Marco Rubio and Ted&nbsp;
Cruz at the very last minute tried to get into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:58.140
dirt with Donald Trump, but it was far too late&nbsp;
at that point. They should have tried to do it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:04.020
earlier if they were going to take down the king&nbsp;
in Republican circles. And if DeSantis decides not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:09.540
to run, if he announces he is not going to enter&nbsp;
the race, we can just wrap this thing up. Right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:14.280
Donald Trump is going to have the nomination&nbsp;
easily in 2024. Spare me if you think that Nikki&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:20.040
Haley or Mike Pompeo or Asa Hutchinson is going&nbsp;
to be able to put a stop to that. And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:23.880
Republicans are only shooting themselves in the&nbsp;
foot by doing this, by choosing Trump, since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:29.160
Trump does not match up very well against Biden&nbsp;
or any other Democrat in the general election,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:34.440
DeSantis does a lot better than Trump does against&nbsp;
a Democrat in the general election, and Trump is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:38.880
going to have the stench of criminality now on him&nbsp;
that he didn't necessarily have in prior election&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:43.380
cycles. Plus, there's these other things that he&nbsp;
has to worry about, like the Capitol insurrection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:49.260
This will be the first time that Trump is on&nbsp;
the ballot since January six, 2021. So that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:54.000
probably not going to play well amongst general&nbsp;
election voters. So there's this real Stockholm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:58.227
syndrome going on within the Republican Party&nbsp;
where even if other Republican figures and right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.227 --> 00:05:03.960
wing voters know that he's bad for the party, he&nbsp;
lost them elections in 2018 and 2020 and 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:09.000
and he's probably poised to do it again in&nbsp;
2024. Even though they know all that stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:13.440
everyone's still too afraid to cross him. And&nbsp;
I guess they just love his personality so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:17.820
that they'll be willing to support him even&nbsp;
when the evidence shows that he is a loser&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:21.720
and he's only hurting the party. So that's the&nbsp;
latest on the polling. Trump seems to be running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:27.180
away with this thing at the moment, and we'll&nbsp;
see if Ron DeSantis even decides to get into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:31.380
this race because it's. Not looking so good for&nbsp;
him at the moment. All right. We're going to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:36.420
to a break right now. We'll be back with much&nbsp;
more of the David Pakman show right after this.

